[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No Location C isolation is present. End-hosts in 192.168.200.0/24 can reach their default gateway and other sites.

Evidence:
- From pc11 (192.168.200.11), ping to its gateway 192.168.200.1 succeeds with 0% loss and ~0.7 ms RTT; ARP shows the gateway MAC resolved and REACHABLE. This confirms local L2 and L3 to the gateway are healthy.
- Topology-wide probes show all four Location C hosts (pc11–pc14) can reach a remote host on another site (pc4, 172.16.10.4) with 0% loss, proving routing beyond the local subnet works.
- On router4 (Location C gateway), interface eth0 is up with 192.168.200.1/24 and matches the MAC seen by pc11. Router4’s routing table has a working default via 172.25.1.2 and multiple remote prefixes learned (RIP), indicating upstream connectivity.

Affected component: none; all subsystems (L2 access, router4 L3 gateway, and upstream routing) are operating normally. Mechanism: Successful pings and valid ARP/neighbors demonstrate end-to-end reachability, contradicting the reported “totally cut off” state. Localization: Location C access path through switch5/switch6 to router4 eth0 is up; router4’s upstream on eth5 is also up with routes installed. Root cause: No outage found; the report likely reflects a transient condition or a host-specific problem not reproduced during testing. Corrective fix: No network changes required. Ask users to retest; if individual hosts still fail, investigate per-host issues (NIC state, IP/DNS settings, local firewall), but the Location C network segment is healthy. 
=== END ===